  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nAs the upper trough deepens and pushes east out of the region, it\ncontinues to push a cooling airmass southeastward. We'll be \nseeing much below average temperatures this morning with lows in \nthe 40s. An isolated dip into the upper 30s will not be out of the\nquestion across the north. \n\nLonger term, ensembles fully exit the trough to the east by \nWednesday allowing a new ridge to establish over the Pacific \nNorthwest. This will slowly extend increasing height rises and\nlowering RH values over eastern Montana with northwest flow \naloft, bringing a steady increase in temps and generally dry\nweather. Highs Thursday will reach up to low 90s and look to last\ninto the beginning of the next week. Friday looks to be the \nhottest day at this time. The current ridge crest looks like it \nmay pass the region around Sunday, which could introduce increased\nchances for rain thereafter. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nDeviations from NBM included using the HRRRSmoke data in for Vis,\nmostly to remove erroneous NBM/GLAMP lowered visibilities.\n\nGAH",
